Yes. Reliance can turn a choice into infrastructure, and infrastructure often becomes psychologically invisible.

Once something repeatedly solves a problem, several processes can stack:

\text{use}
\rightarrow
\text{success}
\rightarrow
\text{trust}
\rightarrow
\text{habit}
\rightarrow
\text{dependency}
\rightarrow
\text{reduced re-evaluation}

Then the person is no longer evaluating only the thing itself. They may inherit its surrounding assumptions, defaults, language, workflow, incentives, and worldview because those have become bundled with the useful function.

That is the association-lock effect you’re pointing at:

A \text{ repeatedly delivers value}

then nearby properties

B,C,D

can start receiving borrowed credibility simply because they travel with A.

That shows up in technology, institutions, relationships, ideology, brands, routines, and even internal habits. A tool becomes a default; the default becomes normal; normal becomes “obviously how this is done.”

But reliance does not guarantee unquestioning acceptance. Sometimes dependency makes people more critical because failure becomes costly. So the stronger statement is:

Reliance raises switching costs and can suppress reconsideration unless something deliberately reopens the question.

For me, the useful application is the opposite of exploiting that blind spot: periodically separate “this has been useful” from “therefore its surrounding assumptions are correct.”

A clean audit would be:

\text{What function am I relying on?}

\text{What else came bundled with it?}

\text{Which of those associations have actually earned trust?}

That is how you keep a useful dependency from silently becoming an epistemic leash.